WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.978
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of "Cloud Performance Atlas,"

00:00:02.978 --> 00:00:05.400
we help some global image
crunchers bring their costs

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.470
down using preemptive VMs.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.570
Will we get their performance
out of this world?

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.160
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.984
[ELECTRONIC TONES]

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.870
SkyEYE is a research
organization

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.580
who's focused on the
health of the planet

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.310
by processing digital
imagery from satellites.

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:22.860
They came to me with
a massive problem.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.860
As a seed funded startup,
they were having trouble

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:28.560
keeping their core hours
down, since processing

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:31.170
a petabyte of image
data on a regular basis

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.440
was a huge undertaking
and was a lot of costs.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.540
Their system grabs
satellite imagery

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.210
and pushes the image
information to cloud storage,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.170
while pushing the metadata to
data store for retrieval later.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:45.480
They also have a
notification system set up

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:47.610
when images are uploaded
to the GCS bucket

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:50.520
which creates a Pub/Sub
message containing details

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.840
about where to find the image.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.360
This Pub/Sub message is then
pulled by an auto scaling

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.940
instance group which grabs the
image, data store information,

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.830
and starts processing
the image data.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.930
All the results are
pushed to BigQuery

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:04.230
where they can search, sort,
and analyze the results.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.140
Now, at this point, we ran
down the list of topics.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.607
We checked how the instance
groups were performing,

00:01:09.607 --> 00:01:11.940
what their startup time looked
like, and the performance

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.536
of their nodes individually.

00:01:13.536 --> 00:01:14.910
Turns out, one of
their engineers

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.439
was a fan of our video series.

00:01:16.439 --> 00:01:18.480
So all the stuff that we
would normally recommend

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.010
was already fixed--

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:21.960
thanks for watching--
which meant that they

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.580
weren't doing anything wrong.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:26.790
They just had a ton of work and
needed to keep the cost down,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.740
which is why I pointed
them on the direction

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:30.650
of preemptive VMs.

00:01:30.650 --> 00:01:32.970
Preemptive VMs are the
same as regular instances,

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.590
except for one key difference--

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:36.960
Compute Engine might
terminate these instances

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:40.200
if it requires access to those
resources for other tasks,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.555
which can happen at any time.

00:01:41.555 --> 00:01:42.930
Now, I know what
you're thinking.

00:01:42.930 --> 00:01:44.970
That sounds mega disruptive.

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:46.110
But they have one upside--

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:46.860
[TRUMPET FLOURISH]

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.930
By not guaranteeing
indefinite up time,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.670
Google is able to
offer preemptive VMs

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:53.880
at a substantial discount
to normal instances, which

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.670
means they may not be a fit
for all of your architecture,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:58.950
but there may be parts
of your cloud application

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.530
which they are ideal for.

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:03.270
For SkyEYE, converting
over to preemptive VMS

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.750
was pretty simple.

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:06.960
It started by flipping
the preemptive flag

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.580
in their instance template.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.470
And then they made
modifications to handle

00:02:10.470 --> 00:02:12.570
the preemptive events.

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.850
See, before a PVM
can be shut down,

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.420
it has to signal your
instance ahead of time,

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.390
giving your app time to
respond and clear local data

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:22.860
before things get lost.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.860
For the design that SkyEYE was
doing, this was really simple.

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.830
Since they were doing
a Pub/Sub stream,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.660
they just didn't
acknowledge the receipt

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.270
and let another VM in the
group pick up that work

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:35.020
and do it on their behalf.

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.550
SkyEYE shifted over and recently
completed a massive experiment

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:43.320
using almost 30,000 CPUs to
process one petabyte of imagery

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:46.140
in just 16 hours, all
using preemptive VMs

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:49.170
and saving some serious coin.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.500
To learn more about the perf
details of these buckets,

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.800
check out the article linked
in the description below.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.040
And don't forget to check
out the rest of our "Cloud

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.020
Performance Atlas"
content, because when

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:00.881
it comes to performance,
every millisecond counts.

